.TH BENCH 3
.SH NAME
bench \- microsecond time, for measuring
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #b /dev

.B /dev/busec

.B include \&"bench.m";
.B bench := load Bench Bench->PATH;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Bench
is the native kernel's apparatus for timing things.
.IR Sys-millisec (2)
is the only clock Limbo otherwise has, and a millisecond is far too coarse
for a machine that runs millions of instructions inside one.
.PP
.B Busec
is a read-only file holding the number of microseconds since the zero
point, as decimal text with no newline.
It is read from the processor's free-running counter
.RI ( fastticks ),
not from the scheduler's tick, and is monotonic.
It is the clock on which
.IR gpio (3)
stamps edges.
.PP
The device also provides the built-in module
.BR $Bench ,
declared in
.BR /module/bench.m ,
which is the interface Vita Nuova's
.I "Reliable Benchmarking with Limbo on Inferno"
is written against, so that benchmarks written to it run here unchanged:
.TP
.B microsec()
The same number as
.BR busec ,
as a
.BR big ,
without a system call's worth of text conversion.
.TP
.B reset()
Make now the zero point.
Times are relative to it rather than to an epoch so that they are small
enough to read and subtract.
.TP
.B "disablegc()\fR, \fPenablegc()"
Stop the garbage collector from running inside a sample, and let it run
again.
A collection is not part of what is being timed, and presents as an
outlier indistinguishable from a real one.
The two nest: they keep a count, and a program that forgets the second
suspends collection only until it exits.
.TP
.B read()
Declared by the module and present so that programs written to it load;
it returns 0.
The original used it to hand back a trace from its built-in benchmarks,
which are not here.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/port/devbench.c
.br
.B /module/bench.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sys-millisec (2),
.IR gpio (3)
.SH BUGS
Native kernel only; the hosted emulator has no
.BR #b .
.SH HISTORY
Inferno's other native kernels have a
.B devbench
that reads the x86 timestamp counter and carries a suite of benchmarks
besides.
This keeps the module's interface and none of the rest.
